          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following lymph node characteristics on palpation is most suggestiv</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following lymph node characteristics on palpation is most suggestive of metastatic carcinoma?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Soft, tender, and fluctuant</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Soft, tender, fluctuant nodes indicate acute pyogenic infection. Tenderness reflects active inflammation, not malignant infiltration.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Rubbery, non-tender, and mobile</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Rubbery, mobile, non-tender nodes are the hallmark of lymphoma, particularly Hodgkin lymphoma. An intact capsule keeps the node mobile.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hard, non-tender, and fixed to surrounding structures</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Hardness and fixity indicate capsular breach and invasion of surrounding tissues by carcinomatous deposits, distinguishing metastasis from lymphoma or infection.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Matted, firm, with central softening</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Matted, firm nodes with central softening suggest tuberculous lymphadenitis with caseation breaking through the capsule.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the consistency discriminator: hard and fixed = metastatic carcinoma; rubbery and mobile = lymphoma; matted and firm = tuberculosis; soft and tender = acute infection."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Hard, fixed, non-tender nodes point to metastatic carcinoma — the hardness reflects desmoplastic reaction and the fixity reflects capsular breach with invasion into surrounding structures."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
