        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 351.15774999999996,
            "to": 361.15774999999996
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In the pathogenesis of tuberculous lymphadenitis, epithelioid histiocytes fuse t</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In the pathogenesis of tuberculous lymphadenitis, epithelioid histiocytes fuse to form Langhans giant cells. What is the distinguishing nuclear arrangement of a Langhans giant cell?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Nuclei scattered randomly throughout the cytoplasm</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Randomly scattered nuclei are the hallmark of foreign-body giant cells, not Langhans giant cells. Foreign-body giant cells form around inert material without T-cell-mediated activation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Nuclei arranged in a peripheral horseshoe or wreath</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Langhans giant cells characteristically arrange their nuclei in a peripheral horseshoe or wreath at the cell edge, formed by IFN-γ-driven fusion of epithelioid histiocytes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Two mirror-image nuclei with prominent eosinophilic nucleoli</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Two mirror-image nuclei with owl-eye nucleoli describe the Reed-Sternberg cell of Hodgkin lymphoma, not a Langhans giant cell of tuberculosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>A single large nucleus with an irregular, lobulated outline</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A single lobulated nucleus describes the lacunar variant of the Reed-Sternberg cell seen in nodular sclerosis Hodgkin lymphoma, not a tuberculous giant cell.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Langhans giant cells arrange their nuclei in a peripheral horseshoe or wreath. Foreign-body giant cells show randomly scattered nuclei. This distinction is a key histological marker in tuberculous granulomas."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The peripheral horseshoe arrangement is the defining feature of Langhans giant cells. This pattern results from the ordered fusion of IFN-γ-activated epithelioid histiocytes in the Type IV hypersensitivity response to Mycobacterium tuberculosis."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
