        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 714.8160416666667,
            "to": 724.8160416666667
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>On examining a cervical lymph node biopsy at high power, you identify a cell mea</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>On examining a cervical lymph node biopsy at high power, you identify a cell measuring approximately 30 micrometres, with two mirror-image nuclei each bearing a prominent eosinophilic nucleolus surrounded by a clear halo. This cell constitutes less than 5 per cent of the cellular population. Which immunohistochemistry profile confirms its identity?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>CD20 positive, CD15 negative, CD30 negative</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CD20+/CD15−/CD30− is the profile of the popcorn (LP) cell in nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin lymphoma, which lacks the classic binucleate owl-eye morphology.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>CD45 positive, CD20 positive, CD3 negative</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CD45+/CD20+ describes a normal or reactive B lymphocyte. The Reed-Sternberg cell is characteristically CD45 negative, which distinguishes it from surrounding reactive lymphocytes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>CD15 positive, CD30 positive, CD45 negative</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The classic Reed-Sternberg cell is CD15+, CD30+, and CD45−. Despite its B-cell origin, it has lost most B-cell surface markers. CD45 negativity separates it from the surrounding reactive infiltrate.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>CD3 positive, CD30 positive, CD15 negative</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CD3+/CD30+ would suggest anaplastic large cell lymphoma, a T-cell neoplasm. The classic Reed-Sternberg cell is CD3 negative and CD15 positive.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The classic Reed-Sternberg cell's immunophenotype is CD15+, CD30+, CD45−. CD45 negativity is the key feature that separates this neoplastic cell from the surrounding reactive lymphocytes that make up 95–99 per cent of the tumour."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The owl-eye Reed-Sternberg cell — CD15+, CD30+, CD45− — is the diagnostic hallmark of classical Hodgkin lymphoma. It constitutes only 1 to 5 per cent of the tumour mass amidst a dense reactive infiltrate."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
